IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hcc-dates`

-- Health Care Complaints Act 1993 (NSW) -- the provisions a person meets who complains to the Health Care Complaints
-- Commission about a health practitioner or a health organisation: what a complaint can be about and who is the
-- complainant, how it is made, who is told of it, withdrawing it, the assessment and its deadlines, when the Commission
-- must investigate and when it may discontinue, notice of the decision and the review the complainant may ask for,
-- notice of the outcome of an investigation, conciliation, protection for complaining, the offences that protect a
-- complainant, and whether the Commission's material can be used in the complainant's own court case.
--
-- SEAT: the complainant (s 4): the person who makes the complaint, or the client (the patient) on whose behalf a
-- representative the client chose makes it; with them the client whose treatment is complained of and the client's
-- contact person or family (s 28A). Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Can I complain about
-- this?", "Will they be told who I am?", "By when must the Commission decide?", "Must it investigate?", "Can it drop my
-- complaint?", "Can I ask for a review, and by when?", "Must I go to conciliation, and can I bring a lawyer?", "Can
-- what was said at conciliation be used in my negligence case?", "Can I be sued for complaining?". The Commission, the
-- Director of Proceedings, the Registrar, the professional councils and the Health Secretary get no seat: what they
-- must do is encoded as what the complainant is owed and by when.
--
-- Provisions: s 4 (complainant, health practitioner, health organisation, relevant health organisation, parties);
-- Part 2 Division 1 (ss 7-9), s 11, s 13(1), Division 3 (ss 16-18), Division 4 (ss 20(2), 21, 22, 23, 25A(2), 25B(2),
-- 27, 28, 28A), ss 39, 41, 42, 45 (outcomes and notice), Division 8 (ss 47, 48, 50, 51, 52, 56), s 58D, s 90CA,
-- ss 96(2), 97A(d), 98, 99, 99A(2)-(3) and the penalties of ss 58, 97A, 98, 99, 99A. Out, with reasons: NOTES.md,
-- "Scope".
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "only if", "unless" or "on", a flat "must not", "does not require", "is not
-- admissible", "may not be compelled", a period the Act fixes); or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on the facts
-- given. Each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment. Judgments the Act leaves to the Commission or a court ("it
-- appears to the Commission", "on reasonable grounds", "sufficient reason", "good faith") are asked as inputs and
-- answered conditionally.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 31 May 2024; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Care Complaints Act 1993`

§§ `The nouns`

-- s 7(1): what a complaint may concern. (a) "the professional conduct of a health practitioner" (including a breach of
-- the Public Health Act 2010 Part 7 Division 1 or 3 or its code of conduct); (a1) "an alleged breach by a relevant
-- health organisation of Division 1 or 3 of Part 7 of the Public Health Act 2010 or of a code of conduct prescribed
-- under section 100(1)(c)"; (b) "a health service which affects, or is likely to affect, the clinical management or
-- care of an individual client".
DECLARE `What my complaint is about` IS ONE OF
    `the professional conduct of a health practitioner`
    `a health service that affects or is likely to affect the clinical management or care of an individual client`
    `a breach of Part 7 Division 1 or 3 of the Public Health Act 2010, or its code of conduct, by a health organisation`
    `a health service that does not affect the clinical management or care of any individual client`
    `something else`

-- s 4 "relevant health organisation means a person that is a health organisation other than" (a) a public health
-- organisation, (b) a public hospital (both within the Health Services Act 1997), (c) a private health facility
-- licensed under the Private Health Facilities Act 2007, (d) a prescribed organisation.
DECLARE `The kind of health organisation` IS ONE OF
    `a public health organisation or public hospital under the Health Services Act 1997`
    `a private health facility licensed under the Private Health Facilities Act 2007`
    `an organisation the regulations exclude`
    `any other health organisation`

-- s 4 "complainant": (a) the person making the complaint, except as in (b); (b) "the client on whose behalf a
-- complaint is made if the complaint is made by a person chosen by the client as his or her representative".
DECLARE `How the complaint came to be made` IS ONE OF
    `I made it myself`
    `a representative I chose made it on my behalf, about my care`
    `I made it as the representative the client chose`
    `someone else made it about my care, not as a representative I chose`

-- s 4 "health practitioner means a natural person who provides a health service"; "health organisation means a body
-- that provides a health service (not being a health practitioner)".
DECLARE `Who the complaint is against` IS ONE OF
    `a health practitioner`
    `a health organisation`

-- s 27 and ss 25A(2), 25B(2).
DECLARE `Whether the Commission can stop dealing with my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `the Commission must discontinue, because it referred the complaint to the Health Secretary or a professional council (s 25A(2) or 25B(2))`
    `the Commission must not discontinue under section 27, because a significant issue of public health or safety appears`
    `the Commission may discontinue, for a reason section 27(1) gives`
    `section 27(1) gives no reason on these facts, but its list is not exhaustive (s 27(2))`

-- s 28(1), (8): the action taken or decision made following assessment (s 20(1)).
DECLARE `The assessment decision` IS ONE OF
    `to investigate the complaint`
    `to refer the complaint for conciliation`
    `to deal with the complaint under Division 9 (complaints resolution)`
    `to refer the complaint to the Health Secretary (s 25 or 25A)`
    `to refer the complaint to a professional council or another person or body (s 25B or 26)`
    `to discontinue dealing with the complaint (s 27)`

-- s 28(9).
DECLARE `Whether the Commission must review the assessment decision` IS ONE OF
    `the Commission must review it`
    `the Commission may review it, but need not, because the 28 days have passed`
    `section 28(9) gives no review, because only the complainant may ask`

-- s 28A: who the person asking is.
DECLARE `Who I am in the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `a party to the complaint (the complainant or the person complained about)`
    `the client whose treatment it is, not a party`
    `the client's contact recorded by the hospital or health care facility`
    `another person associated with the client, such as a legal representative of the client or the client's estate`

DECLARE `Whether I am told the outcome of the assessment` IS ONE OF
    `section 28 applies to you instead, as a party`
    `the Commission must use its best endeavours to tell you`
    `the Commission may tell you, if it thinks it appropriate and practicable`
    `the Commission may not tell you under section 28A`
    `section 28A puts no duty on the Commission to tell you`
    `the Commission need not tell you, because of a risk in section 28A(6)`

-- ss 39(1), 42(1): what the Commission does at the end of an investigation.
DECLARE `Outcome after investigation` IS ONE OF
    `refer the complaint to the Director of Proceedings, who decides whether it is prosecuted before a disciplinary body`
    `refer the complaint to the professional council (for example for performance or impairment assessment)`
    `make comments to the health practitioner`
    `make recommendations or comments to the health organisation, with a report to the Health Secretary`
    `terminate the matter`
    `refer the matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions`
    `make a prohibition order or issue a public statement (s 41A)`
    `make a prohibition order or issue a public statement, if it is a relevant health organisation (s 45C)`

-- s 45(2A).
DECLARE `Whether I can have the report on the health organisation` IS ONE OF
    `the Commission may give you a copy, at its discretion`
    `there is no section 42(2) report, because the Commission made no recommendations or comments`
    `section 45(2A) does not provide for giving you a copy`

-- ss 48, 58D.
DECLARE `The process` IS ONE OF
    `conciliation (Division 8)`
    `complaints resolution (Division 9)`

-- s 98.
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `a threat, intimidation or an inducement`
    `refusing to employ me`
    `dismissing me`
    `subjecting me to a detriment`
    `harassing me`
    `something else was done`

-- The offences a complainant meets: as the one protected (ss 58, 98, 99A) or as the one who gives information
-- (ss 97A(d), 99).
DECLARE `The offence` IS ONE OF
    `persuading by threat, intimidation or inducement, or victimising, a complainant (s 98)`
    `knowingly giving the Commission false or misleading information (s 99)`
    `knowingly giving the Commissioner or the Commission's staff false or misleading information about a complaint (s 97A(d))`
    `a conciliator or Registry staff member disclosing information from conciliation (s 58)`
    `improperly disclosing information obtained under the Act (s 99A)`

-- s 99A(3).
DECLARE `The proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `a civil claim, prosecution or other ordinary court or tribunal proceedings`
    `proceedings under the Royal Commissions Act 1923`
    `proceedings before the Independent Commission Against Corruption`
    `proceedings under Part 3 of the Special Commissions of Inquiry Act 1983`
    `an inquiry under the Ombudsman Act 1974`

DECLARE `Whether it was done in time` IS ONE OF
    `done in time`
    `done late`
    `not done, and the time has passed`
    `not done yet, and the time has not passed`
    `no time limit applies, because it was not required`

-- The parties and acts of the regulative rules.
DECLARE `HCC party` IS ONE OF `the Commission`
DECLARE `HCC act` IS ONE OF
    `complete the assessment`
    `give the parties written notice of the assessment decision`

-- The matters that, if they appear to the Commission, oblige it to act (ss 18(2), 23(1)(b), 26(2), 27(3)).
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, d IS A BOOLEAN, e IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `any of the five matters appears` a b c d e IF a OR b OR c OR d OR e

§§ `s 4 and Division 1 -- the right to complain`

-- s 4 "relevant health organisation means a person that is a health organisation other than the following" (a)-(d).
-- A "means" definition: each excluded kind is JUST FALSE.
@export Is the health organisation I complain about a "relevant health organisation" (s 4)?
GIVEN `the kind of health organisation` IS A `The kind of health organisation` @desc What the organisation is. One of: "a public health organisation or public hospital under the Health Services Act 1997", "a private health facility licensed under the Private Health Facilities Act 2007", "an organisation the regulations exclude", "any other health organisation"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it a relevant health organisation` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of health organisation`
    WHEN `any other health organisation` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 7(1) "A complaint may be made under this Act concerning" (a), (a1), (b); s 7(2) "against a health service
-- provider"; s 7(3) "even though, at the time the complaint is made, the health service provider is not qualified or
-- entitled to provide the health service concerned" (so the provider's qualification is not asked). s 8(1) "A complaint
-- may be made by any person", the client, a parent or guardian, a chosen representative (a lawyer included) among
-- them, so who complains is not asked.
@export Can I make a complaint about this to the Health Care Complaints Commission (ss 7, 8)?
GIVEN `what my complaint is about` IS A `What my complaint is about` @desc What the complaint concerns. One of: "the professional conduct of a health practitioner", "a health service that affects or is likely to affect the clinical management or care of an individual client", "a breach of Part 7 Division 1 or 3 of the Public Health Act 2010, or its code of conduct, by a health organisation", "a health service that does not affect the clinical management or care of any individual client", "something else"
      `the kind of health organisation` IS A `The kind of health organisation` @desc Only for a Public Health Act breach by a health organisation, what the organisation is. One of: "a public health organisation or public hospital under the Health Services Act 1997", "a private health facility licensed under the Private Health Facilities Act 2007", "an organisation the regulations exclude", "any other health organisation"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I make a complaint about this` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what my complaint is about`
    WHEN `the professional conduct of a health practitioner` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a health service that affects or is likely to affect the clinical management or care of an individual client` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a breach of Part 7 Division 1 or 3 of the Public Health Act 2010, or its code of conduct, by a health organisation` THEN
        IF `Is it a relevant health organisation` `the kind of health organisation` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind: limit in the grant's own wording (s 7(1)(a1) "relevant health organisation"), no "only" (fork F2)
        ELSE NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind: limit in the grant's own wording (s 7(1)(b) "which affects ... the clinical management or care of an individual client") (fork F2)
    WHEN `a health service that does not affect the clinical management or care of any individual client` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind: list not closed with "only" (s 7(1)) (fork F2)
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 4 "complainant means (a) the person making a complaint, except as provided by paragraph (b), or (b) the client on
-- whose behalf a complaint is made if the complaint is made by a person chosen by the client as his or her
-- representative for the purpose of making the complaint." A "means" definition.
@export Am I the complainant, with the complainant's rights under the Act (s 4)?
GIVEN `how the complaint came to be made` IS A `How the complaint came to be made` @desc How the complaint was made. One of: "I made it myself" (as the client, a parent or guardian, a relative, a provider or anyone else, but not as the client's chosen representative), "a representative I chose made it on my behalf, about my care", "I made it as the representative the client chose", "someone else made it about my care, not as a representative I chose"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I the complainant` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how the complaint came to be made`
    WHEN `I made it myself` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a representative I chose made it on my behalf, about my care` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 9(1) "A complaint is made by lodging the complaint in writing with the Commission." s 11: a complaint a
-- professional council notifies to the Commission under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law "is taken to
-- have been made in accordance with this Act to the Commission". The note to s 9: "a complaint cannot be acted on
-- until it is put in writing".
@export Has my complaint been made, so that the Commission can act on it (ss 9(1), 11)?
GIVEN `I lodged my complaint in writing with the Commission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have lodged your complaint in writing with the Commission (a phone call or a conversation alone is not enough)
      `a professional council notified the Commission of my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you complained to a professional council (for example the Medical Council of NSW) under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law and the council notified the Commission of it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Has my complaint been made` MEANS
    IF `I lodged my complaint in writing with the Commission` OR `a professional council notified the Commission of my complaint`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 9(3) "It is the duty of staff of the Commission to help a person to make a complaint if the person requests
-- assistance to do so."
@export Must the Commission's staff help me make my complaint (s 9(3))?
GIVEN `I asked for help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the Commission's staff for help in making your complaint
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Commission's staff help me make my complaint` MEANS
    IF `I asked for help` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Division 3 -- who is told of the complaint, and withdrawing it`

-- s 16(1) "The Commission must give written notice of the making of a complaint, the nature of the complaint and the
-- identity of the complainant to the person against whom the complaint is made." s 16(4) "This section does not require
-- the Commission to give notice ... if it appears to the Commission, on reasonable grounds, that the giving of the notice
-- will or is likely to (a) prejudice the investigation of the complaint, or (b) place the health or safety of a client at
-- risk, or (c) place the complainant or another person at risk of intimidation or harassment." s 16(5) "Despite
-- subsection (4), the Commission must give the notice if" natural justice makes it essential, or it is necessary to
-- investigate effectively, or otherwise in the public interest. s 16(6): some other form of notice may be given.
@export Must the person I complain about be told of my complaint and who I am (s 16)?
GIVEN `the Commission finds a section 16(4) risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission, on reasonable grounds, that telling the person will or is likely to prejudice the investigation, put a client's health or safety at risk, or put you or someone else at risk of intimidation or harassment
      `the Commission finds notice essential or necessary anyway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission considers, on reasonable grounds, that notice is essential for natural justice, or necessary to investigate effectively, or otherwise in the public interest
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the person I complain about be told of my complaint and who I am` MEANS
    IF `the Commission finds a section 16(4) risk` AND (NOT `the Commission finds notice essential or necessary anyway`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 16(1) "The notice must be given not later than 14 days after the Commission's assessment of the complaint".
@export If the person I complain about must be told, by what day must the Commission tell them (s 16(1))?
GIVEN `the day the Commission assessed my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission completed its assessment of your complaint
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must the person I complain about be told` MEANS
    `the day the Commission assessed my complaint` `plus days` 14

-- s 16(7) "On the expiration of each consecutive period of 60 days after the complaint is assessed, the Commission must
-- undertake a review of a decision not to give notice" unless notice has been given or the complaint discontinued.
@export If the Commission decided not to tell the person I complain about, on what day must it first review that decision (s 16(7))?
GIVEN `the day the Commission assessed my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission completed its assessment of your complaint
GIVETH A DATE
`When must the Commission first review its decision not to tell the person` MEANS
    `the day the Commission assessed my complaint` `plus days` 60

-- s 16A(1) the Commission "must give written notice of the making of a complaint, the nature of the complaint and the
-- identity of the complainant to a person who currently employs or engages the health practitioner concerned as a
-- health practitioner if the Commission considers on reasonable grounds that the giving of the notice is necessary (a)
-- to assess the matter effectively, or (b) to protect the health or safety of the public or a member of the public."
-- s 16A(2) "does not require" it if the notice will put the complainant or another person at risk of intimidation or
-- harassment, or unreasonably prejudice the practitioner's employment. No current employer: fork F3.
@export Must the health practitioner's employer be told of my complaint and who I am (s 16A)?
GIVEN `the practitioner is currently employed or engaged as a health practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone currently employs or engages the practitioner you complain about as a health practitioner
      `the Commission finds the notice necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission considers, on reasonable grounds, that telling the employer is necessary to assess the matter effectively or to protect the health or safety of the public
      `the Commission finds a section 16A(2) risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission, on reasonable grounds, that telling the employer will put you or someone else at risk of intimidation or harassment, or unreasonably prejudice the practitioner's employment
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the practitioner's employer be told of my complaint and who I am` MEANS
    IF `the practitioner is currently employed or engaged as a health practitioner` AND `the Commission finds the notice necessary` AND (NOT `the Commission finds a section 16A(2) risk`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 17 "On receiving a complaint against a health organisation, the Commission must give written notice of the making
-- of the complaint, the nature of the complaint and the identity of the complainant to the Health Secretary." No
-- exception. "On" read as "when" (ruling of 2026-11-05 (c)): a complaint against a practitioner is JUST FALSE for s 17.
@export Must the Health Secretary be told of my complaint and who I am (s 17)?
GIVEN `who the complaint is against` IS A `Who the complaint is against` @desc Who the complaint is against. One of: "a health practitioner" (a natural person who provides a health service), "a health organisation" (a body that provides a health service)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Health Secretary be told of my complaint and who I am` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who the complaint is against`
    WHEN `a health organisation` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 18(1) "A complainant may withdraw the complainant's complaint at any time (a) by written notice to the Commission,
-- or (b) if the Commission considers it appropriate to accept the withdrawal of the complaint orally -- by oral notice".
@export Can I withdraw my complaint (s 18(1))?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant: the person who made the complaint, or the client on whose behalf a chosen representative made it
      `I withdraw in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you withdraw by written notice to the Commission (rather than orally)
      `the Commission accepts an oral withdrawal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you withdraw orally, whether the Commission considers it appropriate to accept that
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I withdraw my complaint` MEANS
    -- NOTHING-kind: limit in the grant's own wording ("A complainant may"), no "only" (fork F4)
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I am the complainant`) THEN NOTHING
           IF `I withdraw in writing` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF `the Commission accepts an oral withdrawal` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 18(2) "On the withdrawal of a complaint, the Commission may cease to deal with it but must continue to deal with the
-- matter the subject of the complaint if it appears to the Commission that" (a)-(e).
@export If I withdraw my complaint, must the Commission still deal with the matter (s 18(2))?
GIVEN `a significant issue of public health or safety appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the matter raises a significant issue of public health or safety
      `a significant question about a client's care or treatment appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the matter raises a significant question as to the appropriate care or treatment of a client by a health service provider
      `if substantiated there would be grounds for disciplinary action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the matter, if substantiated, would provide grounds for disciplinary action against a health practitioner or relevant health organisation
      `if substantiated it would be gross negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the matter, if substantiated, would involve gross negligence by a health practitioner
      `if substantiated it would be a Public Health Act offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the matter, if substantiated, would result in a finding of guilt of an offence under Part 7 Division 1 or 3 of the Public Health Act 2010
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`If I withdraw, must the Commission still deal with the matter` MEANS
    IF `any of the five matters appears` `a significant issue of public health or safety appears` `a significant question about a client's care or treatment appears` `if substantiated there would be grounds for disciplinary action` `if substantiated it would be gross negligence` `if substantiated it would be a Public Health Act offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Division 4 -- assessment`

-- s 20(2) "Unless the Commission decides to decline to entertain a complaint, the Commission is, as part of its
-- assessment ... (a) to identify the specific allegations ... and (b) to use its best endeavours to confirm with the
-- complainant ... that the matters so identified accord with the information provided by them."
@export Must the Commission check with me the allegations it has identified in my complaint (s 20(2))?
GIVEN `the Commission declined to entertain my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has decided to decline to entertain your complaint
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Commission check the allegations with me` MEANS
    IF `the Commission declined to entertain my complaint` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 21 "the Commission may require the complainant to provide further particulars of the complaint within such time,
-- not exceeding 60 days, as may be specified by the Commission." Counted from the requirement (fork F5).
@export Is the time the Commission gave me to provide further particulars within the 60 days the Act allows (s 21)?
GIVEN `the day the Commission required further particulars` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission required you to give further particulars of your complaint
      `the day the Commission specified` IS A DATE @desc The day by which the Commission said the particulars must be provided
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the time for further particulars within the limit` MEANS
    IF `the days from` `the day the Commission required further particulars` `to` `the day the Commission specified` AT MOST 60
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 22 "The Commission must carry out its assessment of a complaint (a) within 60 days after receiving the complaint, or
-- (b) if, under section 21, the Commission has required the complainant to provide further particulars of the
-- complaint, within 60 days after the date by which the Commission specified that those particulars were to be
-- provided."
GIVEN `the day the Commission received my complaint` IS A DATE, `the Commission required further particulars` IS A BOOLEAN, `the day the Commission specified for the particulars` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the 60 days run from` `the day the Commission received my complaint` `the Commission required further particulars` `the day the Commission specified for the particulars` MEANS
    IF `the Commission required further particulars`
    THEN `the day the Commission specified for the particulars`
    ELSE `the day the Commission received my complaint`

@export By what day must the Commission finish assessing my complaint (s 22)?
GIVEN `the day the Commission received my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission received your written complaint
      `the Commission required further particulars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission required you, under section 21, to give further particulars of the complaint
      `the day the Commission specified for the particulars` IS A DATE @desc If it did, the day by which it said the particulars were to be provided (any day otherwise)
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must the Commission finish assessing my complaint` MEANS
    (`the day the 60 days run from` `the day the Commission received my complaint` `the Commission required further particulars` `the day the Commission specified for the particulars`) `plus days` 60

-- The s 22 duty as a regulative rule. Time 0 is the day the 60 days run from; the unit is days; an assessment on the
-- 60th day is in time.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `HCC party` `HCC act`
`s 22 -- the Commission must carry out its assessment within 60 days` MEANS
    PARTY `the Commission`
    MUST `complete the assessment`
    WITHIN 60
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the Commission` BECAUSE "the assessment of the complaint was not carried out within 60 days (s 22)"

@export Has the Commission assessed my complaint in time (s 22)?
GIVEN `the day the Commission received my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission received your written complaint
      `the Commission required further particulars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission required you, under section 21, to give further particulars of the complaint
      `the day the Commission specified for the particulars` IS A DATE @desc If it did, the day by which it said the particulars were to be provided (any day otherwise)
      `the Commission has completed its assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has completed its assessment of your complaint
      `the day the assessment was completed` IS A DATE @desc If it has, the day it completed it (any day otherwise)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today)
GIVETH A `Whether it was done in time`
`Has the Commission assessed my complaint in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 22 -- the Commission must carry out its assessment within 60 days` 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `done in time`
    OTHERWISE
        BRANCH IF `the Commission has completed its assessment` THEN `done late`
               IF `the days to today` GREATER THAN 60 THEN `not done, and the time has passed`
               OTHERWISE `not done yet, and the time has not passed`
    WHERE
        `the start` MEANS `the day the 60 days run from` `the day the Commission received my complaint` `the Commission required further particulars` `the day the Commission specified for the particulars`
        `the days to today` MEANS `the days from` `the start` `to` `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `the Commission has completed its assessment`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the Commission` `complete the assessment` (`the days from` `the start` `to` `the day the assessment was completed`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL `the days to today`)

-- s 13(1) "If either the Commission or the appropriate professional council is of the opinion that a complaint (or any
-- part of a complaint) should be investigated, it must be investigated." s 23(1) "The Commission must investigate a
-- complaint (a) if, under section 13(1), the appropriate professional council is of the opinion that the complaint
-- should be investigated, or (b) if, following assessment of the complaint, it appears to the Commission that the
-- complaint" (i)-(v). s 23(4): it may investigate despite any agreement between the parties. Where none holds, s 23
-- does not require an investigation (fork F6); the Commission may still choose one (s 20(1)).
@export Must the Commission investigate my complaint (ss 13(1), 23(1))?
GIVEN `the Commission or the professional council thinks it should be investigated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission or the appropriate professional council (for a registered practitioner) is of the opinion that the complaint should be investigated
      `a significant issue of public health or safety appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the complaint raises a significant issue of public health or safety
      `a significant question about a client's care or treatment appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the complaint raises a significant question as to the appropriate care or treatment of a client by a health service provider
      `if substantiated there would be grounds for disciplinary action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the complaint, if substantiated, would provide grounds for disciplinary action against a health practitioner or relevant health organisation
      `if substantiated it would be gross negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the complaint, if substantiated, would involve gross negligence by a health practitioner
      `if substantiated it would be a Public Health Act offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the complaint, if substantiated, would result in a finding of guilt of an offence under Part 7 Division 1 or 3 of the Public Health Act 2010
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Commission investigate my complaint` MEANS
    IF `the Commission or the professional council thinks it should be investigated`
       OR `any of the five matters appears` `a significant issue of public health or safety appears` `a significant question about a client's care or treatment appears` `if substantiated there would be grounds for disciplinary action` `if substantiated it would be gross negligence` `if substantiated it would be a Public Health Act offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 27(1)(f) "the complaint (or part) relates to a matter which occurred more than 5 years before the complaint was
-- made". A matter on the same day of the month five years before is not "more than" five years before (fork F1).
@export Did the matter I complain of happen more than 5 years before I made my complaint (s 27(1)(f))?
GIVEN `the day the matter happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the treatment or other matter you complain of happened
      `the day I made my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged your complaint in writing with the Commission
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Did the matter happen more than 5 years before my complaint` MEANS
    `the day the matter happened` `is before` (`the day I made my complaint` `plus years` (0 MINUS 5))

-- s 27(1) "Following the assessment, the Commission may discontinue dealing with a complaint (or any part of a
-- complaint) for any one or more of the following reasons" (a)-(h). s 27(2) "This section does not exhaust the
-- circumstances". s 27(3) "The Commission must not discontinue dealing with a complaint (or part) under this section if
-- it appears to the Commission that the complaint (or part) raises a significant issue of public health or safety."
-- ss 25A(2), 25B(2) "Despite section 27(3), the Commission must discontinue dealing with a complaint (or part) under
-- that section that has been referred" to the Health Secretary (s 25A) or a professional council (s 25B). s 27(4): a
-- discontinued complaint is terminated. s 97: legal proceedings do not stop the Commission, but s 27(1)(c) lets it
-- discontinue where the subject matter "has been or is the subject of legal proceedings" (finding 3).
@export Can the Commission stop dealing with (discontinue) my complaint (ss 25A(2), 25B(2), 27)?
GIVEN `it was referred to the Health Secretary or a professional council under section 25A or 25B` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission referred the complaint to the Health Secretary for an inquiry (s 25A) or to a professional council (s 25B)
      `a significant issue of public health or safety appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission that the complaint raises a significant issue of public health or safety
      `it is frivolous, vexatious or not in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission finds the complaint frivolous, vexatious or not made in good faith
      `it is trivial or does not warrant action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission finds the subject matter trivial, or not warranting investigation, conciliation or complaints resolution
      `it is or was investigated elsewhere or before a court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subject matter has been or is under investigation by another competent person or body, or has been or is the subject of legal proceedings (for example your own negligence claim)
      `the Commission referred it to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission referred the complaint to another person or body for investigation or other action (such as performance or impairment assessment)
      `I had another satisfactory way and no sufficient reason not to use it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is or was a satisfactory alternative way for you to deal with the matter and the Commission finds you have no sufficient reason for not pursuing it
      `the day the matter happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the treatment or other matter you complain of happened
      `the day I made my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged your complaint in writing with the Commission
      `I had a sufficient reason for the delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the matter happened more than 5 years before your complaint, whether the Commission accepts you had a sufficient reason for the delay
      `I failed to give further particulars in time without sufficient reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed, without sufficient reason, to give the further particulars the Commission asked for within the time it set
      `it is a Commonwealth matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint concerns a matter within the responsibility of the Commonwealth
GIVETH A `Whether the Commission can stop dealing with my complaint`
`Can the Commission discontinue my complaint` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `it was referred to the Health Secretary or a professional council under section 25A or 25B` THEN `the Commission must discontinue, because it referred the complaint to the Health Secretary or a professional council (s 25A(2) or 25B(2))`
           IF `a significant issue of public health or safety appears` THEN `the Commission must not discontinue under section 27, because a significant issue of public health or safety appears`
           IF `a reason in section 27(1)` THEN `the Commission may discontinue, for a reason section 27(1) gives`
           OTHERWISE `section 27(1) gives no reason on these facts, but its list is not exhaustive (s 27(2))`
    WHERE
        `a reason in section 27(1)` MEANS
               `it is frivolous, vexatious or not in good faith`
            OR `it is trivial or does not warrant action`
            OR `it is or was investigated elsewhere or before a court`
            OR `the Commission referred it to someone else`
            OR `I had another satisfactory way and no sufficient reason not to use it`
            OR (`Did the matter happen more than 5 years before my complaint` `the day the matter happened` `the day I made my complaint` AND (NOT `I had a sufficient reason for the delay`))
            OR `I failed to give further particulars in time without sufficient reason`
            OR `it is a Commonwealth matter`

-- s 28(1) "The Commission must give the parties to the complaint notice in writing of the action taken or decision made
-- by the Commission following its assessment of the complaint. The notice is to be given within 14 days after the
-- Commission takes that action or makes that decision."
@export By what day must the Commission tell me in writing what it decided after assessing my complaint (s 28(1))?
GIVEN `the day the Commission made its decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission took the action or made the decision following its assessment
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must I be told the assessment decision` MEANS
    `the day the Commission made its decision` `plus days` 14

-- The s 28(1) duty as a regulative rule. Time 0 is the day of the decision; the unit is days; notice on the 14th day is
-- in time. s 28(4) "This section does not require the Commission to give notice of action taken or a decision made to
-- investigate a complaint if it appears to the Commission, on reasonable grounds, that the giving of the notice will"
-- (a) prejudice the investigation, (b) put a client's health or safety at risk, (c) put the complainant or another
-- person at risk of intimidation or harassment, (d) unreasonably prejudice an employed practitioner's employment;
-- s 28(5): unless natural justice, effective investigation or the public interest requires it.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `HCC party` `HCC act`
`s 28(1) -- the Commission must give the parties written notice within 14 days of its assessment decision` MEANS
    PARTY `the Commission`
    MUST `give the parties written notice of the assessment decision`
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the Commission` BECAUSE "the parties were not given written notice of the assessment decision within 14 days (s 28(1))"

@export Was I told in writing of the Commission's assessment decision in time (s 28(1), (4))?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The assessment decision` @desc What the Commission decided after assessing your complaint. One of: "to investigate the complaint", "to refer the complaint for conciliation", "to deal with the complaint under Division 9 (complaints resolution)", "to refer the complaint to the Health Secretary (s 25 or 25A)", "to refer the complaint to a professional council or another person or body (s 25B or 26)", "to discontinue dealing with the complaint (s 27)"
      `the Commission finds a section 28(4) risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a decision to investigate: whether it appears to the Commission, on reasonable grounds, that notice will prejudice the investigation, put a client's health or safety at risk, put you or someone else at risk of intimidation or harassment, or unreasonably prejudice an employed practitioner's employment
      `the Commission finds notice essential or necessary anyway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission considers, on reasonable grounds, that notice is essential for natural justice, or necessary to investigate effectively, or otherwise in the public interest
      `the day the Commission made its decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission took the action or made the decision following its assessment
      `I have been told in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given written notice of the decision
      `the day I was told` IS A DATE @desc If you have, the day of the written notice (any day otherwise)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today)
GIVETH A `Whether it was done in time`
`Was I told of the assessment decision in time` MEANS
    IF `notice is not required`
    THEN `no time limit applies, because it was not required`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 28(1) -- the Commission must give the parties written notice within 14 days of its assessment decision` 0 `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `done in time`
         OTHERWISE
             BRANCH IF `I have been told in writing` THEN `done late`
                    IF `the days to today` GREATER THAN 14 THEN `not done, and the time has passed`
                    OTHERWISE `not done yet, and the time has not passed`
    WHERE
        `notice is not required` MEANS
                `the decision` EQUALS `to investigate the complaint`
            AND `the Commission finds a section 28(4) risk`
            AND (NOT `the Commission finds notice essential or necessary anyway`)
        `the days to today` MEANS `the days from` `the day the Commission made its decision` `to` `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `I have been told in writing`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the Commission` `give the parties written notice of the assessment decision` (`the days from` `the day the Commission made its decision` `to` `the day I was told`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL `the days to today`)

-- s 28(8) "The Commission's notice to the parties to the complaint must include (a) advice that the complainant may ask
-- the Commission to review the decision made after assessing the complaint if the decision is (i) not to investigate
-- the complaint, or (ii) to refer the complaint to the Health Secretary under section 25 or 25A, or (iii) to refer the
-- complaint to another person or body under section 25B or 26, or (iv) to discontinue dealing with the complaint under
-- section 27, and (b) the reasons for the decision." Every decision but investigation is "not to investigate".
@export Must the Commission's notice of its assessment decision tell me I can ask for a review (s 28(8)(a))?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The assessment decision` @desc What the Commission decided after assessing your complaint. One of: "to investigate the complaint", "to refer the complaint for conciliation", "to deal with the complaint under Division 9 (complaints resolution)", "to refer the complaint to the Health Secretary (s 25 or 25A)", "to refer the complaint to a professional council or another person or body (s 25B or 26)", "to discontinue dealing with the complaint (s 27)"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the notice tell me I can ask for a review` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `to investigate the complaint` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 28(9) "The Commission may review a decision made after assessing a complaint if requested to do so by the
-- complainant, and must do so if the request is made within 28 days after the complainant is notified of the
-- decision." The 28th day is within (fork F1).
@export By what day must I ask for a review of the assessment decision, so that the Commission must review it (s 28(9))?
GIVEN `the day I was notified of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the Commission's decision after assessing your complaint
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must I ask for a review of the assessment decision` MEANS
    `the day I was notified of the decision` `plus days` 28

@export If I ask, must the Commission review its decision after assessing my complaint (s 28(9))?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant: the person who made the complaint, or the client on whose behalf a chosen representative made it
      `the day I was notified of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified of the Commission's decision after assessing your complaint
      `the day I ask for a review` IS A DATE @desc The day you ask (or will ask) the Commission to review the decision
GIVETH A `Whether the Commission must review the assessment decision`
`Must the Commission review its assessment decision` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I am the complainant`) THEN `section 28(9) gives no review, because only the complainant may ask`
           IF (`By what day must I ask for a review of the assessment decision` `the day I was notified of the decision`) `is before` `the day I ask for a review` THEN `the Commission may review it, but need not, because the 28 days have passed`
           OTHERWISE `the Commission must review it`

-- s 28A(1) the Commission "is to use its best endeavours to give notification of the outcomes of the assessment of a
-- complaint to a client whose treatment is the subject of the complaint and who is not required to be given notice under
-- section 28 unless the client (a) is deceased, or (b) is incapable of understanding the notification." (2) for treatment
-- at a hospital or facility, best endeavours to tell "any person recorded ... as being a contact for the client". (3) it
-- "may, if it thinks it appropriate and it is practicable", tell any associated person, a legal representative of the
-- client or estate included. (4) "The Commission may only give notification to a person under subsection (2) or (3) if
-- the client concerned (a) is deceased, or (b) is incapable of understanding the notification and the client's
-- authorised representative ... has consented". (6) "does not require" notice in the s 28(4) risks; (7) unless natural
-- justice, effective investigation or the public interest requires it.
@export Is the Commission to tell me the outcome of the assessment of a complaint about a client's treatment (s 28A)?
GIVEN `who I am in the complaint` IS A `Who I am in the complaint` @desc Who you are. One of: "a party to the complaint (the complainant or the person complained about)", "the client whose treatment it is, not a party", "the client's contact recorded by the hospital or health care facility", "another person associated with the client, such as a legal representative of the client or the client's estate"
      `the client has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the client whose treatment is complained of has died
      `the client cannot understand the notification` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the client is incapable of understanding the notification
      `the client's authorised representative consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the client is incapable, whether the client's authorised representative (Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002, s 8) has consented to the Commission telling you
      `the Commission finds a section 28A(6) risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the Commission, on reasonable grounds, that telling you will prejudice the investigation, put a client's health or safety at risk, put someone at risk of intimidation or harassment, or unreasonably prejudice an employed practitioner's employment
      `the Commission finds notice essential or necessary anyway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission considers, on reasonable grounds, that notice is essential for natural justice, or necessary to investigate effectively, or otherwise in the public interest
GIVETH A `Whether I am told the outcome of the assessment`
`Is the Commission to tell me the outcome of the assessment` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who I am in the complaint`
    WHEN `a party to the complaint (the complainant or the person complained about)` THEN `section 28 applies to you instead, as a party`
    WHEN `the client whose treatment it is, not a party` THEN
        BRANCH IF `the client has died` OR `the client cannot understand the notification` THEN `section 28A puts no duty on the Commission to tell you`
               IF `the risk excuses notice` THEN `the Commission need not tell you, because of a risk in section 28A(6)`
               OTHERWISE `the Commission must use its best endeavours to tell you`
    WHEN `the client's contact recorded by the hospital or health care facility` THEN
        BRANCH IF (NOT `subsection (4) allows it`) THEN `the Commission may not tell you under section 28A`
               IF `the risk excuses notice` THEN `the Commission need not tell you, because of a risk in section 28A(6)`
               OTHERWISE `the Commission must use its best endeavours to tell you`
    WHEN `another person associated with the client, such as a legal representative of the client or the client's estate` THEN
        BRANCH IF (NOT `subsection (4) allows it`) THEN `the Commission may not tell you under section 28A`
               IF `the risk excuses notice` THEN `the Commission need not tell you, because of a risk in section 28A(6)`
               OTHERWISE `the Commission may tell you, if it thinks it appropriate and practicable`
    WHERE
        `subsection (4) allows it` MEANS
            `the client has died` OR (`the client cannot understand the notification` AND `the client's authorised representative consented`)
        `the risk excuses notice` MEANS
            `the Commission finds a section 28A(6) risk` AND (NOT `the Commission finds notice essential or necessary anyway`)

§§ `Divisions 6 and 7 -- the outcome of an investigation`

-- s 39(1) "At the end of the investigation of a complaint against a health practitioner, the Commission must do one or
-- more of the following" (a) refer to the Director of Proceedings, (c) refer to the professional council, (d) comment
-- to the practitioner, (e) terminate, (f) refer to the DPP, (g) act under s 41A. s 42(1) for a health organisation:
-- (a) terminate, (b) recommendations or comments (with a report to the Health Secretary, s 42(2)), (c) refer to the
-- DPP, (d) act under s 45C. Section 39 says "one or more"; s 42 says "or" (finding 5).
@export What can the Commission do at the end of investigating my complaint (ss 39(1), 42(1))?
GIVEN `who the complaint is against` IS A `Who the complaint is against` @desc Who the complaint is against. One of: "a health practitioner" (a natural person who provides a health service), "a health organisation" (a body that provides a health service)
GIVETH A LIST OF `Outcome after investigation`
`What can the Commission do at the end of the investigation` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who the complaint is against`
    WHEN `a health practitioner` THEN
        LIST `refer the complaint to the Director of Proceedings, who decides whether it is prosecuted before a disciplinary body`,
             `refer the complaint to the professional council (for example for performance or impairment assessment)`,
             `make comments to the health practitioner`,
             `terminate the matter`,
             `refer the matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions`,
             `make a prohibition order or issue a public statement (s 41A)`
    WHEN `a health organisation` THEN
        LIST `terminate the matter`,
             `make recommendations or comments to the health organisation, with a report to the Health Secretary`,
             `refer the matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions`,
             `make a prohibition order or issue a public statement, if it is a relevant health organisation (s 45C)`

-- s 41(1) after s 39 and s 40, the Commission "must notify the parties to the complaint ... in writing, of the results of
-- the investigation, the action taken under section 39 and the reasons for taking that action and include advice that
-- the complainant may ask the Commission to review the decision". s 45(1) "it must notify the parties to the complaint,
-- in writing, of the results of the investigation" (s 45(4): including the action and the reasons). s 4 "parties to a
-- complaint means the complainant and the person against whom the complaint is made". Someone else may be told at the
-- Commission's discretion (ss 41(2), 45(2)).
@export Must the Commission tell me in writing the results of its investigation of my complaint, with its reasons (ss 41(1), 45(1))?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant: the person who made the complaint, or the client on whose behalf a chosen representative made it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be told the results of the investigation` MEANS
    IF `I am the complainant`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind: the Act leaves it to the Commission's discretion (ss 41(2), 45(2)); not a "no"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 41(3) "The Commission must review a decision made under section 39 if asked to do so by the complainant." No time
-- limit (finding 4). Division 7 (health organisations) has no review provision: NOTHING (finding 4, fork F7).
@export If I ask, must the Commission review what it decided to do at the end of the investigation (s 41(3))?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant: the person who made the complaint, or the client on whose behalf a chosen representative made it
      `who the complaint is against` IS A `Who the complaint is against` @desc Who the complaint is against. One of: "a health practitioner", "a health organisation"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Commission review its decision after the investigation` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who the complaint is against`
    WHEN `a health practitioner` THEN
        IF `I am the complainant` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind: silent (Division 7 gives no review of a s 42 decision) (fork F7)
    WHEN `a health organisation` THEN NOTHING

-- s 45(2A) "The Commission may, at its discretion, also give a copy of a report prepared under section 42(2) on the
-- matter the subject of the complaint to the complainant." s 42(2): a report is prepared only if the Commission makes
-- recommendations or comments. s 45(3): the report is not otherwise released.
@export Can I be given a copy of the Commission's report on the health organisation I complained about (s 45(2A))?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant
      `who the complaint is against` IS A `Who the complaint is against` @desc Who the complaint is against. One of: "a health practitioner", "a health organisation"
      `the Commission made recommendations or comments to the organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the end of its investigation, the Commission made recommendations or comments to the health organisation
GIVETH A `Whether I can have the report on the health organisation`
`Can I have the report on the health organisation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I am the complainant`) OR (`who the complaint is against` EQUALS `a health practitioner`) THEN `section 45(2A) does not provide for giving you a copy`
           IF (NOT `the Commission made recommendations or comments to the organisation`) THEN `there is no section 42(2) report, because the Commission made no recommendations or comments`
           OTHERWISE `the Commission may give you a copy, at its discretion`

-- s 90CA "The Director of Proceedings may give notice of the Director's determination as to whether or not a complaint
-- should be prosecuted before a disciplinary body to ... (a) the parties to the complaint". A power, not a duty: the
-- answer is that the Director may, never that the Director must (finding 6).
@export May the Director of Proceedings tell me whether my complaint will be prosecuted before a disciplinary body (s 90CA)?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant (a party to the complaint)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May the Director of Proceedings tell me whether my complaint will be prosecuted` MEANS
    IF `I am the complainant`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind: s 90CA(c) reaches others through s 45; not asked here
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Division 8 -- conciliation, and Division 9 -- complaints resolution (only if the complaint is referred to them)`

-- s 47 "Within 14 days after the referral of a complaint by the Commission to the Health Conciliation Registry, the
-- Registrar must give written notice to the parties" that it was referred, that it is voluntary, its objects,
-- confidentiality, the effect of agreements, and why conciliation is appropriate.
@export By what day must the Registrar tell me in writing that my complaint was referred for conciliation (s 47)?
GIVEN `the day of the referral` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission referred your complaint to the Health Conciliation Registry
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must the Registrar tell me about the conciliation` MEANS
    `the day of the referral` `plus days` 14

-- s 48 "Participation in the conciliation process by the parties to a complaint is voluntary." s 58D likewise for
-- complaints resolution under Division 9.
@export Must I take part in conciliation or complaints resolution (ss 48, 58D)?
GIVEN `the process` IS A `The process` @desc The process. One of: "conciliation (Division 8)", "complaints resolution (Division 9)"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I take part` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the process`
    WHEN `conciliation (Division 8)` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `complaints resolution (Division 9)` THEN JUST FALSE

-- s 50(1) "At the conciliation of a complaint, a party to the complaint is not entitled to be legally represented."
-- s 50(2) "A party to a complaint may be assisted by another person (not being an Australian legal practitioner) if the
-- assistance is provided (a) to the complainant, or (b) to another party to the complaint and the Registrar or
-- conciliator gives permission because the party would be disadvantaged without the assistance." s 50(4): a
-- contravention does not invalidate the conciliation. A lawyer as helper: JUST FALSE (fork F8).
@export May this person help me at the conciliation (s 50)?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant (rather than the person complained about)
      `the helper is a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who would help you is an Australian legal practitioner
      `the Registrar or conciliator gave permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are not the complainant, whether the Registrar or conciliator gave permission because you would be disadvantaged without help
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May this person help me at the conciliation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the helper is a lawyer` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `I am the complainant` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF `the Registrar or conciliator gave permission` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 51(1) "Evidence of anything said or of any admission made during the conciliation process is not admissible in any
-- proceedings before a court, tribunal or body." (2) the same for a document prepared for or in it. (3) "This section
-- does not apply ... if the persons who attended, or were named during, the conciliation process and, in the case of a
-- document, all persons named in the document, consent". (4) a person cannot be required by subpoena to produce it.
@export Can what was said or written at the conciliation be used as evidence in court, for example in my negligence claim (s 51)?
GIVEN `everyone who attended or was named consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether everyone who attended or was named during the conciliation (and, for a document, everyone named in it) consents to its use as evidence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can what was said at conciliation be used as evidence` MEANS
    IF `everyone who attended or was named consented` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 52(1) "The conciliation process is concluded (a) if either party terminates the conciliation process at any time,
-- or (b) if the parties to the complaint reach agreement". (3) it "is terminated if the conciliator terminates the
-- process after having formed the view" that agreement is unlikely or that a significant issue of public health or
-- safety has been raised.
@export Has the conciliation ended (s 52(1), (3))?
GIVEN `a party ended it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or the other party terminated the conciliation
      `we reached agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties reached agreement about the matter complained of
      `the conciliator ended it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conciliator terminated it, having formed the view that agreement is unlikely or that a significant issue of public health or safety has been raised
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Has the conciliation ended` MEANS
    IF `a party ended it` OR `we reached agreement` OR `the conciliator ended it` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 52(2) "The complainant must notify the Registrar without delay if the parties reach agreement otherwise than during
-- the conciliation process." "Without delay" sets no number of days, so no deadline is computed.
@export Must I tell the Registrar that we reached agreement outside the conciliation (s 52(2))?
GIVEN `I am the complainant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the complainant
      `we reached agreement outside the conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after the referral to conciliation, the parties reached agreement otherwise than during the conciliation process
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I tell the Registrar about our agreement` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I am the complainant`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `we reached agreement outside the conciliation` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- s 56(1) "Despite section 20A, the Commission may investigate a complaint that has been dealt with under this Division
-- but only if (a) the report under section 53 contains a recommendation that the Commission investigate the complaint,
-- or (b) new material ... becomes available and that material raises a matter that would cause the Commission to refer
-- the complaint for investigation in accordance with section 23." s 23(4): an agreement between the parties does not by
-- itself stop an investigation.
@export After conciliation, can the Commission still investigate my complaint (s 56(1))?
GIVEN `the conciliator's report recommends investigation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conciliator's report under section 53 recommends that the Commission investigate the complaint
      `new material raises a section 23 matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether new material has become available that raises a matter that would require investigation under section 23 (for example a significant public health or safety issue, or possible gross negligence)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commission investigate after conciliation` MEANS
    IF `the conciliator's report recommends investigation` OR `new material raises a section 23 matter` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Part 7 -- protection for complainants, offences, and the Commission's information`

-- s 96(2) "The making of a complaint, or the reporting of any matter or thing that could give rise to a complaint, to
-- the Commission or a professional council by any person does not, if it was done in good faith, subject the person
-- personally to any action, liability, claim or demand." Good faith is the court's finding.
@export Am I protected from being sued for making my complaint (s 96(2))?
GIVEN `I complained in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the complaint (or reported the matter) in good faith, as a court would find
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I protected from being sued for complaining` MEANS
    IF `I complained in good faith` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 98(1) "A person who, by threat, intimidation or inducement, persuades or attempts to persuade another person (a) not
-- to make a complaint ... or not to continue with a complaint ..., or (b) not to have discussions with, or take part in
-- proceedings before, the Commission or a professional council ... is guilty of an offence." s 98(2) "A person who
-- refuses to employ or dismisses another person, subjects another person to any detriment, or harasses another person,
-- because the other person (a) intends to make a complaint, has made a complaint, or has had a complaint made on his or
-- her behalf or otherwise concerning him or her ..., or (b) intends to take part, is taking part, or has taken part in
-- any discussions with, or proceedings before," the Commission or a council, "is guilty of an offence." Outside these
-- words s 98 makes no offence (fork F9).
@export Was what was done to me because of my complaint an offence under section 98?
GIVEN `what was done to me` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What the person did. One of: "a threat, intimidation or an inducement", "refusing to employ me", "dismissing me", "subjecting me to a detriment", "harassing me", "something else was done"
      `it was to stop me complaining or taking part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a threat, intimidation or inducement: whether it was to persuade you not to make or continue a complaint, or not to talk to or take part in proceedings before the Commission or a professional council
      `it was because of a complaint or my taking part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For the other acts: whether it was because you intended to complain, complained, had a complaint made on your behalf or about you, or intended to take, are taking or took part in discussions or proceedings with the Commission or a professional council
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it an offence under section 98` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what was done to me`
    WHEN `a threat, intimidation or an inducement` THEN
        IF `it was to stop me complaining or taking part` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `something else was done` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE
        IF `it was because of a complaint or my taking part` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 99 "A person who furnishes the Commission with information for the purposes of this Act knowing that it is false or
-- misleading in a material particular is guilty of an offence." s 97A(d) the same for information given to "an
-- authorised person, the Commissioner or a member of staff of the Commission ... in relation to a complaint".
@export Could I commit an offence by what I tell the Commission about my complaint (ss 97A(d), 99)?
GIVEN `I know it is false or misleading in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information you give the Commission or its staff is, to your knowledge, false or misleading in a material way
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could I commit an offence by what I tell the Commission` MEANS
    IF `I know it is false or misleading in a material particular` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- Maximum penalties: s 98 "200 penalty units or imprisonment for 12 months"; s 99 "200 penalty units"; s 97A "200
-- penalty units"; s 58 "10 penalty units or imprisonment for 6 months, or both"; s 99A(1) "10 penalty units or
-- imprisonment for 6 months, or both". The dollar value of a penalty unit is set by another Act (not deposited), so the
-- answer is in penalty units.
@export What is the largest fine, in penalty units, for this offence (ss 58, 97A, 98, 99, 99A)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence. One of: "persuading by threat, intimidation or inducement, or victimising, a complainant (s 98)", "knowingly giving the Commission false or misleading information (s 99)", "knowingly giving the Commissioner or the Commission's staff false or misleading information about a complaint (s 97A(d))", "a conciliator or Registry staff member disclosing information from conciliation (s 58)", "improperly disclosing information obtained under the Act (s 99A)"
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the largest fine in penalty units` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `persuading by threat, intimidation or inducement, or victimising, a complainant (s 98)` THEN 200
    WHEN `knowingly giving the Commission false or misleading information (s 99)` THEN 200
    WHEN `knowingly giving the Commissioner or the Commission's staff false or misleading information about a complaint (s 97A(d))` THEN 200
    WHEN `a conciliator or Registry staff member disclosing information from conciliation (s 58)` THEN 10
    WHEN `improperly disclosing information obtained under the Act (s 99A)` THEN 10

-- The prison term, in months; 0 where the penalty the section sets is a fine only (fork F10).
@export What is the longest prison term, in months, for this offence (0 if the section sets a fine only) (ss 58, 97A, 98, 99, 99A)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence. One of: "persuading by threat, intimidation or inducement, or victimising, a complainant (s 98)", "knowingly giving the Commission false or misleading information (s 99)", "knowingly giving the Commissioner or the Commission's staff false or misleading information about a complaint (s 97A(d))", "a conciliator or Registry staff member disclosing information from conciliation (s 58)", "improperly disclosing information obtained under the Act (s 99A)"
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest prison term in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `persuading by threat, intimidation or inducement, or victimising, a complainant (s 98)` THEN 12
    WHEN `knowingly giving the Commission false or misleading information (s 99)` THEN 0
    WHEN `knowingly giving the Commissioner or the Commission's staff false or misleading information about a complaint (s 97A(d))` THEN 0
    WHEN `a conciliator or Registry staff member disclosing information from conciliation (s 58)` THEN 6
    WHEN `improperly disclosing information obtained under the Act (s 99A)` THEN 6

-- s 99A(2) "A person may not be compelled in any legal proceedings to give evidence about, or produce documents
-- containing, any information obtained in exercising a function under this Act." (3) subsection (2) does not apply to
-- Royal Commissions, ICAC, Part 3 special commissions, Ombudsman inquiries. In those, the bar is lifted but the Act does
-- not say the material must be produced: NOTHING. s 99B(1)(b), (i): the Commission may still choose to disclose to a
-- court or to the client (finding 7).
@export Can the Commission or its staff be compelled to give evidence of, or produce, what they learned about my complaint in court proceedings (s 99A(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the proceedings` IS A `The proceedings` @desc The proceedings. One of: "a civil claim, prosecution or other ordinary court or tribunal proceedings", "proceedings under the Royal Commissions Act 1923", "proceedings before the Independent Commission Against Corruption", "proceedings under Part 3 of the Special Commissions of Inquiry Act 1983", "an inquiry under the Ombudsman Act 1974"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commission be compelled to give its information in proceedings` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the proceedings`
    WHEN `a civil claim, prosecution or other ordinary court or tribunal proceedings` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind: points outside the sources (the bar is lifted; whether production can be compelled is for the other Act)
    OTHERWISE NOTHING
